{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a methodological rule for minimizing risk of error, not an inductive inference of future success. It costs the agent the comfort of believing the chosen theory is 'probably true,' accepting instead that it is merely the 'least refuted' option available for critical application.","counterconditions":["If the agent believes the corroboration report implies a higher probability of future survival.","If the agent uses the preferred theory to justify ignoring further testing.","If the distinction between 'fitness for action' and 'likelihood of truth' collapses in practice."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Reframes 'preference' from a probabilistic bet on truth to a procedural commitment to the theory that has withstood the most severe attempts at falsification. Action is taken on the basis that if a theory has survived severe tests where others failed, it is the rational choice for provisional reliance, without asserting it will continue to survive.","optional_refs":["63ad735fce4055d4a81bea986a33c237343101f9945358996ba91111fd98909f","c361c4eaac2a55f2a71f100cdf0c863c5ff9bb7226426ac202dd03d0b4fe0b60"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference smuggles induction back in because any decision to act on a corroborated theory implicitly assumes that the future will resemble the past (that the conditions of the test will hold), which is the very principle Popper rejected.","counterconditions":["If Popper can define a form of 'rational expectation' that is distinct from 'inductive probability'.","If the agent can act purely on the basis of eliminating falsehoods without any positive expectation of the survivor.","If the 'cost' of this view is admitting that science offers no guidance for action, only for criticism."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Argues that 'acting on' a theory necessarily entails a predictive expectation of its reliability. Even if labeled 'methodological,' the practical utility of corroboration vanishes unless one assumes the structural stability of the world that allowed the theory to survive previously.","optional_refs":["b854f658248e1c9b4c0c679104b34a989765ec17fcef37e0eff18837fc9704f8","11dcfafffd03651fd56a823bd820b88ce33aeb923bddd85bb054c246c9d1f553"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible only if we accept a 'decisionist' interpretation: the choice of which theory to act on is a non-rational, existential leap, guided but not determined by corroboration. This saves Popper from induction but costs science its claim to rational superiority in practical matters.","counterconditions":["If the 'leap' can be shown to be rationally constrained rather than arbitrary.","If Popper explicitly endorsed decisionism over critical justification.","If the cost of 'non-rational' choice is acceptable within the scientific ethos."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Posits that corroboration provides the *context* for decision, but the *act* of preferring is a voluntary commitment (a 'leap') that logic cannot justify. This aligns with Popper's later emphasis on critical rationalism as an attitude rather than a proof.","optional_refs":["1ddb259890880286201f3a8df51e325138d9168d6972226ed48d300872dd7ffa","242c582b625fdcd71d0c7da09725240184f2d911792836944aea613e91fe5f09"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45}],"simulation_proposals":[]}